The code statements that lead to the bug are:
sig = kvmemdup_bpfptr(usig, attr->signature_size);
Reason:
There was no size limit check on `attr->signature_size` before passing it to `kvmemdup_bpfptr()`. This allowed a user to supply an excessively large value, forcing the kernel to allocate memory via `kmalloc_large` or `vmalloc`, which could be abused for denial-of-service or resource exhaustion.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (attr->signature_size > KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE)
    return -EINVAL;
Reason:
This check rejects signature sizes larger than `KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE`, which corresponds to the maximum size that `kmalloc` can handle without falling back to expensive allocation paths (`kmalloc_large` or `vmalloc`). By enforcing this limit, the fix prevents abuse while still allowing all practical BPF signature sizes.

